---
/**
 * The real app, one scroll below the hero.
 *
 * This is one of the six captures written into src/assets/shots by
 * `make screenshots`; the other five stay in `Screens.astro`. It is promoted
 * out of that section for two reasons the section itself argues for.
 *
 * `Screens.astro` says the hand-drawn macOS mocks — `MenuBar.astro`,
 * `Grant.astro`, `Activity.astro` — exist "so a reader can check the drawings
 * against it". Left at the eighth section, the thing to check them against
 * arrived five sections AFTER the drawings. It comes first now, so what follows
 * reads as a diagram of something already seen.
 *
 * And `Hero.astro` records that the visual it replaced "put nothing
 * Apple-app-shaped above the fold". The hero card answers that with
 * `SurfaceGlyph` line drawings, which are deliberately not Apple's icons and
 * deliberately do not look like them. This shot is where the reader sees the
 * apps they actually recognise — Mail, Notes, Reminders, Calendar, Contacts,
 * Messages, Safari and Maps in the sidebar, drawn by macOS at capture time
 * because `SurfaceIcon.swift` asks `NSWorkspace` for them. That is the app's
 * own UI photographed, which is why it is allowed here and exporting the same
 * icons into `public/` is not. See the header of `SurfaceGlyph.astro`.
 *
 * The image is IMPORTED, not copied. It is the same file `Screens.astro` reads,
 * and `make screenshots` deletes every .png in that directory on every run —
 * parking a duplicate anywhere else would go stale on the next capture. To
 * change what this shows, change the fixture in
 * apps/apple/Cupertino/DemoSeed.swift and re-run.
 *
 * `#shot-surface` moved here with the figure. The anchor predates this section
 * and still resolves; the `shot-` prefix is what keeps it from colliding with
 * `Activity.astro`'s bare `#activity`.
 */
import { Picture } from "astro:assets";
import surfaceShot from "../assets/shots/surface.png";
import SectionHeader from "./SectionHeader.astro";
---

{/* `py-[96px]` matches `Examples` above. `MenuBar` below carries no top
    padding at all — it borrows its gap from whatever precedes it — so this
    section's bottom padding is what keeps the two apart. */}
<section class="wrap py-[96px]">
  <SectionHeader eyebrow="The app" heading="One pane per<br />surface.">
    Cupertino is a window you can open, not a background process. Each surface gets a pane: whether
    the app can be driven, whether its store can be read, who is talking to it, and what it can
    currently say.
  </SectionHeader>

  {/* No heading in the caption — `SectionHeader` above carries it, and stating
      it twice on one screen reads as a mistake. */}
  <figure id="shot-surface" class="reveal m-0 scroll-mt-24">
    {/* Widths are the rendered CSS widths, not the source's — the shot is
        2240px wide at 2x, so 1120 is the 1x plate and 2240 the retina one.
        avif then webp, with the PNG as the fallback the <picture> keeps. */}
    <Picture
      src={surfaceShot}
      alt="Cupertino's Mail pane: automation allowed, the Envelope Index readable, writes enabled, 20 tools · 3 prompts · 3 resources, and five connected clients."
      widths={[720, 1120, 2240]}
      sizes="(min-width: 1180px) 1120px, 100vw"
      formats={["avif", "webp"]}
      fallbackFormat="png"
      class="block h-auto w-full rounded-[14px] border border-rule-2 shadow-[0_24px_60px_-34px_rgba(0,0,0,.9)]"
    />
    <figcaption class="mt-5 max-w-[76ch] text-[14.5px] leading-[1.65] text-pretty text-fg-dim">
      The tool, prompt and resource count comes from asking the server itself, live — so flipping the
      write toggle above it changes what it reports.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>
